  <section id="translation">
    <h2>Translation</h2>

    <h3>Amin Ayyub Samkoug</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>Personal information</dt>
      <dd>
        Date of birth: the year
        <mark>1903<sup><a href="#n1" id="r1">[1]</a></sup></mark>.
        Date of death: the year 1952 (aged 48–49)
        <sup><a href="#n12" id="r12">[12]</a></sup>.
      </dd>
    </dl>
    <p>
      Amin Ayyub Samkoug (1903–1952) is a Syrian researcher and man of letters,
      one of the notable figures and dignitaries of the Circassians in Syria.
      [ref. 1]
    </p>

    <h3>His life and upbringing</h3>
    <p>
      He was born in the town of al-Mansura in the Syrian Golan in 1903. His
      father, the martyr Ayyub Samkoug, was killed
      <mark>
        while fighting against the Italian occupation of Libya, in Tripoli in
        the west, in 1911, among the Syrians who defended many Arab countries
        throughout modern history<sup><a href="#n2" id="r2">[2]</a></sup>
      </mark>.
      He studied in Quneitra, Damascus and Beirut and went to France to obtain
      <mark>
        the higher certificate in agriculture and world politics in the years
        1921–1929<sup><a href="#n3" id="r3">[3]</a></sup>
      </mark>. [ref. 2]
    </p>
    <p>
      He began his primary schooling in the city of Quneitra, then completed
      his studies at the “Anbar” school in Damascus on a free scholarship from
      the Ottoman government because he was a martyr’s son, as his father was
      killed in Tripoli, Libya, while taking part in its defence against the
      Italian occupation of it. He then obtained the secondary certificate,
      learned French in Beirut for one year, joined the
      <mark>
        Sorbonne University in France<sup><a href="#n3b" id="r3b">[3]</a></sup>
      </mark>
      and obtained a specialization diploma in general agriculture, and also a
      diploma in international politics.
    </p>

    <h3>His life in Paris</h3>
    <p>
      Amin spent
      <mark>
        about ten years in Paris, in the period between 1921 and 1929<sup><a href="#n4" id="r4">[4]</a></sup>
      </mark>,
      which he spent studying to obtain the academic degree first. He also
      worked in a large store in Paris until he could secure the expenses of
      his studies. His presence in Paris was a good chance to give himself over
      to research and digging, in France’s famous libraries, for books,
      references and manuscripts dealing with the history of the Circassian
      nation and its causes.
    </p>

    <h3>His career</h3>
    <p>
      He was
      <mark>
        elected head of the Quneitra municipality, then elected a deputy in the
        Syrian parliament in the years 1931–1932<sup><a href="#n5" id="r5">[5]</a></sup>
      </mark>.
      He helped to issue
      <mark>
        the first Circassian newspaper in Syria<sup><a href="#n6" id="r6">[6]</a></sup>,
      </mark>
      in Arabic, Circassian, French and Turkish, which is the newspaper
      <em>Marj</em>, and he was its
      <mark>
        responsible managing editor; it continued to appear from 1928 to 1931<sup><a href="#n6b" id="r6b">[6]</a></sup>
      </mark>.
      He founded the Circassian school in Quneitra in 1933 and
      <mark>
        took over the teaching in it and its administration himself<sup><a href="#n7" id="r7">[7]</a></sup>
      </mark>.
      He wrote many Circassian books, among them
      <mark>
        the book <em>The Circassian Alphabet</em>, the book <em>Circassian
        Poems</em>, the book <em>The History of the Circassians from Ancient
        Times to the New Age</em>, the book <em>The Circassians in Their Wars
        against the Tsars</em> and the book <em>Introduction to the History of
        the Circassians</em><sup><a href="#n8" id="r8">[8]</a></sup>
      </mark>. [ref. 3]
    </p>

    <h3>His biography</h3>
    <p>
      The Syrian researcher and man of letters Amin Samkoug was well known for
      his continuous struggle in the service of his people and his
      <mark>
        usurped homeland<sup><a href="#n9" id="r9">[9]</a></sup>
      </mark>,
      where he spent all his efforts for this cause. He was a teacher, an
      athlete, an instrumentalist, a composer, an administrator, a writer, a
      researcher, a man of letters and a politician.
      <mark>
        The flame of his burning struggle went out with his death in 1952,
        while he was repeating “Long live my nation”<sup><a href="#n10" id="r10">[10]</a></sup>
      </mark>,
      calling for its continued service and for sending it back into the
      arenas of living history anew.
    </p>

