  <section id="history">
    <h2>How the text developed</h2>
    <p>
      I read the page history through the Wikipedia API. This matters because
      the entry looks more heavily sourced than it was.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>13 November 2008.</strong> The page is created, titled “The
        writer, researcher and man of letters Amin Samkoug”, as a short bulleted
        list: born 1903, his father’s death in 1911, studies in 1921–1929,
        elected mayor then deputy in 1931–1932, <em>Marj</em> 1928–1931, the
        school in 1933, the five books, and the “Long live my nation” ending.
        It cites one source: the book <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">«أعلام الشراكسة»</span>
        (“Notables of the Circassians”) by Faisal Habtoush Khut Abzakh
        (Amman, 2007). The page’s creator has the same name as the book’s
        author, so he may be the author, but I can’t confirm that.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>2010–2012.</strong> Categories (born 1903, died 1952) are added,
        and the page still cites the 2007 book.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>By 3 February 2016.</strong> The cited source has been changed
        to the magazine <span lang="ar" dir="rtl">«البروز»</span> (“Al-Buruz”),
        “published by the Circassian Charitable Society in Damascus, various
        issues”. A bot also tags the page as having no source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>7 March 2024.</strong> A user expands the page from about 2.9 KB
        to about 5.4 KB. The education section and the Paris section are added,
        and the three references appear. The new text matches the
        <a href={blog}>2016 blog post</a> and the <a href={esyria}>2011 eSyria article</a>
        word for word. The 2008 text stays, and the older book reference is gone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>2026.</strong> Bots only (a template removed and an archive
        link added).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <mark>
        Review: so the <strong>core facts</strong> (1903, mayor then deputy in
        1931–32, <em>Marj</em> 1928–31, the school in 1933, the book list and
        the last words) come from the 2008 text, which was based on a 2007 book
        that is no longer cited. The <strong>education and Paris text</strong>
        were copied from eSyria and the blog. This is not an independent
        source, and it does not confirm what those pages say.
      </mark>
      The 2008 list matches the Turkish profile on several points
      (<em>Marj</em> 1928–31, the books), so they probably share a source.
    </p>
  </section>
