  <section id="notes">
    <h2>Things to review</h2>
    <ol class="review">
      <li id="n1">
        <mark>
          <strong>[1] The birth year, 1903.</strong> The eSyria “first mayor”
          article that this page cites (ref. 3) says 1900, which is also what Mirzoev, the dictionary entry and the
          Turkish profiles give. 1903 comes from the 2008 text and matches the
          note on his portrait and the 2016 blog (the
          <a href="/people/amin-samkough/biography/">biography page</a> gives
          1900–1953).
        </mark>
        <a href="#r1">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n2">
        <mark>
          <strong>[2] His father.</strong> “Fighting against the Italians” is
          plain, but eSyria says he died there, and the Turkish profile says he
          fought there in 1911. The remark “among the Syrians who defended many
          Arab countries” is an editorial aside that eSyria doesn’t have. “Martyr”
          and “jihad” are the sources’ words, which I translated as “martyr” and
          “fighting”.
        </mark>
        <a href="#r2">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n3">
        <mark>
          <strong>[3] Studies.</strong> The first paragraph speaks of a “higher
          certificate in agriculture and world politics” in France in
          1921–1929. The next says Sorbonne diplomas. The Turkish profile has
          the schools of Grignon and Sciences Politiques, and Mirzoev has an
          agronomist qualification in Beirut and then the Sorbonne. The Sorbonne
          would be an unusual place for an agriculture diploma.
        </mark>
        <a href="#r3">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n4">
        <mark>
          <strong>[4] Ten years in Paris.</strong> 1921 to 1929 is eight years,
          and this is the same slip as in the 2016 blog, which it was copied
          from. If he was in Paris until 1929, he could not also have been
          mayor of Quneitra from 1928 (Mirzoev) or editor of <em>Marj</em>
          from 1928, as this very page says.
        </mark>
        <a href="#r4">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n5">
        <mark>
          <strong>[5] Mayor, then deputy, “1931–1932”.</strong> The order is
          mayor first. eSyria has the parliament in 1930 and the
          mayoralty in 1937. Mirzoev has the mayoralty from 1928 to 1936 and
          parliament in 1931. “1931–1932” may be a parliamentary term, but
          it isn’t explained, and the word “elected” for the mayoralty is
          different from the “appointed” raised by Kushkhabiev.
        </mark>
        <a href="#r5">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n6">
        <mark>
          <strong>[6] “The first Circassian newspaper in Syria”.</strong> eSyria
          says the first in the diaspora. The claim is unsourced, and it may
          refer to a Circassian-language paper or to any Circassian paper.
        </mark>
        <a href="#r6">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n6b">
        <mark>
          <strong>[6] Editor, and 1928–1931.</strong> “Responsible managing
          editor” is a stronger claim than eSyria’s “helped found, and later
          became editor-in-chief”, and Mirzoev names Hagur Tarik Mumtaz as
          editor-in-chief. Mirzoev gives 1927–1932 for the run, while this page
          gives 1928–1931, the same as the Turkish profile.
        </mark>
        <a href="#r6b">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n7">
        <mark>
          <strong>[7] Taught and ran the school himself.</strong> Mirzoev says
          the first director was Rifat Gutoko, who died in April 1933, and that
          Akif Hunagu then directed the school, with Samkough as one of the
          teachers. The Turkish profile says only that he arranged its opening.
        </mark>
        <a href="#r7">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n8">
        <mark>
          <strong>[8] The books.</strong> They are listed as written, with no
          place or year. eSyria says the book on the wars against the Tsars is
          a manuscript that was lost before printing, and the Turkish profile
          says it was published in Homs in 1948. Mirzoev gives Amman 1984 for it
          and Damascus 1984 for the <em>Introduction</em>.
        </mark>
        <a href="#r8">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n9">
        <mark>
          <strong>[9] “Usurped homeland”</strong>
          (<span lang="ar" dir="rtl">وطنه المغتصَب</span>). The page doesn’t say
          whether this means the Caucasus, Syria or the Golan, which has been
          occupied since 1967. It is the 2007 author’s phrase and I can’t tell
          what he meant.
        </mark>
        <a href="#r9">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n10">
        <mark>
          <strong>[10] The “last words” and the praise.</strong> “Long live my
          nation” is a claim that can’t be checked, and the list of roles
          (athlete, instrumentalist, composer…) has no source. The wording is
          tribute, not documented fact.
        </mark>
        <a href="#r10">↩</a>
      </li>
      <li id="n12">
        <mark>
          <strong>[12] “Aged 48–49”.</strong> This is generated from the two
          years, so it only repeats the uncertainty over 1903.
        </mark>
        <a href="#r12">↩</a>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
